TEXT 48 16 Left 3 ;#328 Single-supply sensor signal conditioner
TEXT 48 80 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OlhN7ADpKds
TEXT 48 128 Left 2 ;Alan's final circuit from page 4: a biased inverting stage that turns a 2.5 V to 0.5 V sensor
TEXT 48 160 Left 2 ;swing into 0 V to 5 V, on a single 5 V rail.
TEXT 48 816 Left 2 !.include ..\..\LTspice\Models\Microchip\MCP6001_family_RevE_Bordodynov.lib
TEXT 48 856 Left 2 !.dc Vsensor 0 3 10m
TEXT 48 776 Left 2 !.options gminsteps=0
TEXT 48 912 Left 2 !.meas dc vout_hi FIND V(conditioned_out) AT 0.5
TEXT 48 952 Left 2 !.meas dc vout_lo FIND V(conditioned_out) AT 2.5
TEXT 48 992 Left 2 !.meas dc voff FIND V(voffset) AT 2.5
TEXT 48 1048 Left 2 ;Every value is Alan's. He picks R1 = 33k, then R2 = R1 x 2.5 = 82k for the gain of -2.5 the
TEXT 48 1080 Left 2 ;mapping needs, works out that the non-inverting input has to sit at 2.5 V x R2 / (R1 + R2) =
TEXT 48 1112 Left 2 ;1.78 V, and gets that from 27k over 15k across the 5 V rail.
TEXT 48 1160 Left 2 ;The MCP6002 he uses is a dual; this is the single-channel MCP6001 from the same family model,
TEXT 48 1192 Left 2 ;which is one channel of it. Rail-to-rail input and output is the reason he chose the part: the
TEXT 48 1224 Left 2 ;output has to reach both rails and the input common-mode range has to include 1.78 V.
